<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basilicata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basilicata is a mountainous region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>, with short coastlines on the Tyrrhenian and Ionian seas. In antiquity most of it was the land of the Lucanians and later part of the Roman region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a>. Its capital is Potenza, and the city of Matera is famous for its ancient cave dwellings, the Sassi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basilicata.jpg" alt="Basilicata" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilicata_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
